2186504400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504557 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504557 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504557 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504557 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504557 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186504476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504557 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ad0c27f9-210c-5ce2-b084-7092323ef65b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504599 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504599 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504599 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504599 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504599 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504599 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504599 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504599 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186504427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504599 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f8cc4165-06fc-5353-97db-60ce6554a423" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504627 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504627 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129552" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504627 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504627 #edb/ref 1071 "At which points on the surface $z=xy-3x+2y-5$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504627 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "540d859a-8179-5e53-b6ff-389c30bf0b61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504627 #edb/ref 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-3x+2y-5)=y-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-3x+2y-5)=x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨y-3,\\,x+2,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}y-3=0 \\\\ x+2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the second equation we get $x=-2,$ while the first equation gives $y=3.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=(-2)(3)-3(-2)+2(3)-5=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(-2,3,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504633 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504633 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129479" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504633 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504633 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w={x}^{2}+yz$ at the point $(-1,1,1,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504633 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5d1f491f-4f62-54c6-804f-2430afabf623" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504633 #edb/ref 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+yz)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+yz)=z \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+yz)=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(-1,1,1) & =2(-1)=-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(-1,1,1) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(-1,1,1) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-2 & =(x-(-1))\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(-1,1,1)+(y-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(-1,1,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(-1,1,1) \\\\ w-2 & =-2(x+1)+(y-1)+(z-1) \\\\ w-2 & =-2x+y+z-4 \\\\ 2x-y-z+w+2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504653 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504653 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73855" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504653 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504653 #edb/ref 1071 "At which points on the surface $z={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504653 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8c4b6aea-97fe-50e3-9ab8-7b3e7d7d950a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504653 #edb/ref 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=0$ while the second equation gives $y=0.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz={e}^{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504673 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504673 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129449" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504673 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504673 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}y$ at the point $(1,0,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504673 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bbe6eb83-f049-5d99-ba2d-54fe9f07632a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504673 #edb/ref 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =-2xy \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =2y-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y)=(1,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,0) & =-2(1)(0)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,0) & =2(0)-{1}^{2}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-0 & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,0)+(y-0)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,0) \\\\ z & =0(x-1)-y \\\\ z & =-y \\\\ y+z & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504691 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504691 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129555" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504691 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504691 #edb/ref 1071 "At which point on the surface $z=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504691 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d52f8b63-948e-53a8-93f0-88d0cdc22d4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504691 #edb/ref 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{x}{y},\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation we get $y=1,$ while the second equation gives $x=0.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=0\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,1,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
